module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularSequence.KoszulAugmentation
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.SheafHomStalk
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.KoszulSheafDifferential
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.KoszulStalkComplexIso
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.StalkwiseExactness
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.FreeSheafStalkBasis
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Reflexive.ExtensionChain.StalkCriterion

/-!
# The stalk of the quotient of the structure sheaf by an ideal generated by a finite family

Let `O♯` be a sheaf of commutative rings on a space `X` and let
`φ : (O♯)ʳ ⟶ O♯` be a morphism of sheaves of modules, so that the quotient `O♯/I` by the ideal
sheaf generated by `φ` is the cokernel of `φ`. This file computes the stalk of that quotient: at
a point `x`,

`(O♯/I)_x ≅ O_x ⧸ J_x`,

where `J_x` is the ideal of the stalk `O_x` which is the image of the stalk map of `φ` — the
ideal generated by the germs at `x` of the sections generating `I`.

The point of the computation is that the right-hand side is a **commutative ring**, obtained from
Mathlib's `Ideal.Quotient`, whereas the left-hand side is only the stalk of a sheaf of modules.
Results about modules over the quotient ring — base change of a Hom, base change of an exterior
power, duality for a finite free module — can therefore be applied to the stalks of the quotient
and of sheaves of modules over it, which is not possible for the sheaf-level quotient without
first making `O♯/I` a sheaf of rings.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.RegularSequence.stalkGermIdeal`: the ideal `J_x`, as the image of the stalk map of `φ`.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.quotientStalkMap`: the surjection `O_x ⟶ (O♯/I)_x`.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.quotientStalkEquiv`: the identification `O_x ⧸ J_x ≅ (O♯/I)_x`.

## Main statements

* `Stacks.RegularSequence.range_stalkLin_eq_ker_stalkLin`: on stalks, the image of `φ` is the
  kernel of the projection to the quotient.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.quotientStalkMap_surjective`: the stalk of a surjection of sheaves of
  modules is surjective.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.ker_quotientStalkMap`: the kernel of `O_x ⟶ (O♯/I)_x` is `J_x`.
* `Stacks.RegularSequence.quotientStalkEquiv_mk`: the identification sends the class of `a` to
  the image of `a`, so it identifies *the canonical map* rather than merely asserting that the
  two modules are isomorphic.

## Implementation notes

**Why the ideal is defined as an image rather than as a span.** `Ideal R` is a reducible
abbreviation for `Submodule R R`, so the image of a linear map into `O_x` *is* an ideal with no
coercion. Defining `J_x` that way makes `ker_quotientStalkMap` a three-step composite — the
kernel of a composite with an isomorphism, the stalkwise image-equals-kernel identity, and a
pushforward rewritten as a pullback along the inverse — with no spanning argument anywhere, and
it makes the statement independent of a choice of generators.

**Why that proof is in term mode.** `TopCat.Sheaf` is a definition rather than a structure, so a
rewrite inside a goal mentioning `koszulQuotient` produces a motive that is not type-correct at
implicit transparency; and `LinearMap.ker_comp` is stated for the semilinear composite, which a
syntactic rewrite does not match against `∘ₗ`. Both obstacles disappear when the identities are
composed as terms, where the required equalities hold up to definitional unfolding.

**The two vocabularies for a stalk map.** The exactness results are stated for
`AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkFunctor`, whose morphisms are `ModuleCat` homomorphisms,
while the identification of the stalks used downstream is in the `modStalk`/`germAt` vocabulary,
whose maps are `Stacks.RegularSequence.stalkLin`. The two maps have the same underlying function,
but they are not interchangeable as *morphisms*: an equality of `LinearMap`s between the two
spellings is not accepted. So exactness is transported at the level of **elements**, through
`ShortComplex.moduleCat_exact_iff`, where the agreement is definitional and applies silently.

**The zero-morphism instance is supplied by hand.** Mapping a `ShortComplex` along the stalk
functor requires `PreservesZeroMorphisms` for that functor. The instance exists
(`AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkFunctor_toRingCat_preservesZeroMorphisms`) but is not
found by instance search at this spelling of the sheaf of rings, so it is introduced into the
local context before the short complex is mapped.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01AG](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01AG)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01AH](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01AH)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Sheaf Opposite TopologicalSpace
open AlgebraicGeometry

universe u

namespace Stacks.RegularSequence

open Stacks.SheafOfModules Stacks.SheafOfModules.SheafHomStalk
open Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ReflexiveModules

variable {X : TopCat.{u}} (Rc : TopCat.Sheaf CommRingCat.{u} X)
  [HasSheafify (Opens.grothendieckTopology X) AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  [(Opens.grothendieckTopology X).WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  {r : ℕ}
  (φ : koszulFreeSheaf Rc r ⟶
    SheafOfModules.unit (toRingCat (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥X) Rc))

/-- **The ideal of germs at `x` of the sections generating `I`.** It is the image of the map
induced on stalks by `φ`, carried into the stalk of the sheaf of rings along the identification
of that stalk with the stalk of the structure sheaf as a module. A submodule of a ring over
itself is an ideal, so this is an ideal with no further construction. -/
def stalkGermIdeal (x : X) : Ideal (ringStalk Rc x) :=
  Submodule.map (unitModuleStalkLinearEquiv Rc.obj x).toLinearMap
    (LinearMap.range (stalkLin φ.val x))

/-- **On stalks, the image of `φ` is the kernel of the projection to the quotient.** The complex
`(O♯)ʳ ⟶ O♯ ⟶ O♯/I` is exact at the middle term, because the third map is the cokernel of the
first, and exactness of sheaves of modules is detected on stalks. -/
theorem range_stalkLin_eq_ker_stalkLin (x : X) :
    LinearMap.range (stalkLin φ.val x)
      = LinearMap.ker (stalkLin (koszulQuotientπ φ).val x) := by
  have _hz :=
    AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkFunctor_toRingCat_preservesZeroMorphisms Rc x
  set S : ShortComplex (SheafOfModules.{u} (toRingCat (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥X) Rc)) :=
    ShortComplex.mk φ (cokernel.π φ) (cokernel.condition φ) with hS
  set F := AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.stalkFunctor
    (toRingCat (Opens.grothendieckTopology ↥X) Rc) x with hF
  have hex : (S.map F).Exact :=
    (AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.exact_iff_stalkFunctor_map_exact S).mp
      (ShortComplex.exact_cokernel φ) x
  have hfe := (ShortComplex.moduleCat_exact_iff (S.map F)).mp hex
  refine Submodule.ext fun y => ⟨?_, fun hy => hfe y hy⟩
  rintro ⟨z, rfl⟩
  rw [LinearMap.mem_ker]
  exact congrFun (congrArg (fun m => ⇑(ModuleCat.Hom.hom m)) (S.map F).zero) z

/-- **The canonical surjection from the stalk of the structure sheaf onto the stalk of the
quotient.** It is the stalk of the projection `O♯ ⟶ O♯/I`, with the stalk of the structure sheaf
written as the stalk of the sheaf of rings. -/
def quotientStalkMap (x : X) :
    ringStalk Rc x →ₗ[ringStalk Rc x] modStalk (koszulQuotient.{u, u, u} φ) x :=
  stalkLin (koszulQuotientπ φ).val x ∘ₗ
    (unitModuleStalkLinearEquiv Rc.obj x).symm.toLinearMap

/-- **The canonical map onto the stalk of the quotient is surjective.** The projection to a
cokernel is an epimorphism, and an epimorphism of sheaves of modules is surjective on every
stalk. -/
theorem quotientStalkMap_surjective (x : X) :
    Function.Surjective (quotientStalkMap Rc φ x) :=
  ((AlgebraicGeometry.SheafOfModules.epi_iff_surjective_stalkMap
      (koszulQuotientπ φ)).1 inferInstance x).comp
    (unitModuleStalkLinearEquiv Rc.obj x).symm.surjective

/-- **The kernel of the canonical map onto the stalk of the quotient is the ideal of germs.** -/
theorem ker_quotientStalkMap (x : X) :
    LinearMap.ker (quotientStalkMap Rc φ x) = stalkGermIdeal Rc φ x :=
  (LinearMap.ker_comp _ _).trans
    ((congrArg (Submodule.comap (unitModuleStalkLinearEquiv Rc.obj x).symm.toLinearMap)
        (range_stalkLin_eq_ker_stalkLin Rc φ x)).symm.trans
      (Submodule.map_equiv_eq_comap_symm _ _).symm)

/-- **The stalk of the quotient of the structure sheaf by an ideal generated by a finite family.**
At a point `x`, the stalk of `O♯/I` is the quotient of the commutative ring `O_x` by the ideal
generated by the germs at `x` of the generating sections. This is the first isomorphism theorem
applied to the surjection `O_x ⟶ (O♯/I)_x`, whose kernel is that ideal.

The source is a quotient of a commutative ring by an ideal, so it carries Mathlib's ring
structure; this is what makes base-change arguments available at the stalks of the quotient. -/
def quotientStalkEquiv (x : X) :
    (ringStalk Rc x ⧸ stalkGermIdeal Rc φ x) ≃ₗ[ringStalk Rc x]
      modStalk (koszulQuotient.{u, u, u} φ) x :=
  (Submodule.quotEquivOfEq _ _ (ker_quotientStalkMap Rc φ x).symm).trans
    (LinearMap.quotKerEquivOfSurjective _ (quotientStalkMap_surjective Rc φ x))

/-- **The identification is the canonical map on classes.** The class of `a` in `O_x ⧸ J_x` goes
to the image of `a` under the stalk of the projection `O♯ ⟶ O♯/I`. This specifies the
identification, rather than merely asserting that the two modules are isomorphic. -/
theorem quotientStalkEquiv_mk (x : X) (a : ringStalk Rc x) :
    quotientStalkEquiv Rc φ x (Submodule.Quotient.mk a) = quotientStalkMap Rc φ x a :=
  rfl

end Stacks.RegularSequence

end
